---
import type { CollectionEntry } from 'astro:content'
import StatusTag from './StatusTag.astro'

/**
 * One project, as one row of `ls -1 projects/`.
 *
 * The row is a `<tr>`, and the file name is the link. `ls` lists directory entries, so
 * the listing shows `project.id` — the markdown file's own name without `.md` — rather
 * than the `title` from its frontmatter. That is the detail that makes the command
 * above it true: `cat src/content/projects/` and the page say the same thing.
 *
 * The human title is still in the accessible name, prefixed as hidden text, so a screen
 * reader hears "Sh_Images Reborn, sh-images-reborn, link" and a sighted reader sees the
 * file name. Label in Name holds: the visible string is a suffix of the name, not a
 * replacement for it.
 *
 * TWO LINKS IN ONE ROW, and why they cannot share a cell. The row used to be the hit
 * target, with the name link's `::after` stretched over it — which is fine until a
 * second destination arrives, because an anchor inside an anchor is invalid HTML and
 * both destinations break in every browser. There is no way to nest, and no `z-index`
 * makes it legal. So the two destinations are two elements:
 *
 *   name    -> the case study, the row's reason for existing
 *   stack   -> the stack, then the project's first outbound link, which is its repository
 *
 * The repository used to be a SOURCE column of its own, and moving it into STACK is a
 * width decision wearing a disguise. The old drop order was `stack -> date -> source`, so
 * the source went first at 380px — below most phones, on the one column that is the only
 * path from a listing to the code. Folding it into STACK without also removing STACK from
 * the drop order would have achieved nothing: both would vanish together, and the change
 * would have been invisible on exactly the screens it was meant to fix. So STACK is no
 * longer droppable and the DATE is the column that gives way. `index.astro` owns the
 * breakpoint and its arithmetic.
 *
 * The cost is honest and worth naming: the row is no longer one large hit target, so the
 * hit area is the name text. In exchange the repository is reachable, which on a
 * job-hunting site is worth more than a bigger target — a recruiter's next click is
 * almost always "let me read the code".
 *
 * Tab order is the DOM order and it is the right one: case study first, because that is
 * what the row is for, then repository. Both are reachable by keyboard, and the repository
 * is in the tab order at EVERY width, because the cell that holds it is the one cell that
 * is never dropped. That is the whole point of the change: the destination survives the
 * narrowest phone in the list, and it does so by not being a column.
 *
 * The name cell is a `<th scope="row">`: it names the row, so it is a header, and the
 * status, the date and the stack are announced against it instead of as a bare list of
 * strings. `th` is not a heading element, so this adds nothing to the page outline.
 */

interface Props {
  project: CollectionEntry<'projects'>
}

const { project } = Astro.props
const { status, date, stack, links } = project.data
const href = `/projects/${project.id}`
const iso = date.toISOString().slice(0, 10)

/**
 * The first outbound link, and the only one rendered here.
 *
 * `links: []` is valid and one entry has it, so the repository has to be able to vanish
 * rather than render an empty or broken anchor. `undefined` is falsy, so the anchor is
 * simply not emitted, and the separator between the stack and the repository is a
 * `margin-inline-start` ON the anchor rather than a character in the template. A
 * character would be a thing that can be emitted without the thing it separates; a margin
 * belongs to the element it separates, so a project with `links: []` cannot leave a
 * dangling separator behind. That is the whole failure mode, closed structurally.
 *
 * First link only, deliberately. A row wide enough for a case study, a repository, a
 * live demo and a changelog is a row that fits on none of the widths this page has to
 * work at, and the rest are one click away on the case study itself.
 */
const source = links[0]

/**
 * The visible label for that link: the href's own last path segment, and nothing else.
 *
 * The rendered cell is 76 shell cells wide and the full four-column row is 656 of them,
 * so the label is a width decision. The author wrote `https://github.com/Shoking01/
 * Sh_Images-Reborn` — 45 characters, of which 29 are the host and the owner. The host is
 * the part that can go: it is the same on every repository, it is what the browser shows
 * in the status bar on hover, and a `/` is a path separator rather than two of them. What
 * is left is `Sh_Images-Reborn`, 16 characters, which is the repository's own name
 * verbatim — recognisable as a repository, one cell shorter than `/Sh_Images-Reborn` and
 * ten shorter than the owner-qualified form.
 *
 * The arithmetic that decided it, in the units the design already measures in (`1ch` is
 * exactly 9px at `--text-base`: 0.9375rem = 15px, JetBrains Mono's advance is 0.6em, so
 * the shell's 76 cells are 684px). NAME is 16 cells because `sh-images-reborn` is 16
 * characters, DATE is 10 because an ISO date is 10, and STACK is sized by this cell:
 *
 *     rust gpui windows  Sh_Images-Reborn
 *      17 cells  1ch gap  16 cells   = 34 cells = 306px
 *
 * so the full row is name 144 + status 62 + date 90 + stack 306 + 3 gutters 54 = 656px
 * against a 684px shell, which leaves 28px. The rejected forms are rejected on those
 * numbers: `/Sh_Images-Reborn` fits at 665px but drops the margin to 19px for a leading
 * slash that buys a readability the underline below already provides, and
 * `Shoking01/Sh_Images-Reborn` is 746px — 62 over the shell, which overflows the page
 * rather than dropping a column. Sixteen characters with 28px to spare is the widest
 * label that still reads as a repository.
 *
 * Derived, not typed, and derived so the invariant cannot rot: the visible string is a
 * suffix of the real `href`, so the accessible name and the visible string agree and
 * nobody can quietly change the label into something the link is not. The one case that
 * breaks the invariant is a trailing slash — `https://x.com/repo/` does not end with
 * `repo` — so the guard is a real check against the untouched `href`, and the author's
 * own `label` is the fallback for a URL that fails it. `links[0].label` still renders as
 * written on the case study page, which has a whole column width to print it in.
 */
const base = source?.href.replace(/\/+$/, '') ?? ''
const segment = base.slice(base.lastIndexOf('/') + 1)
const repoLabel = source ? (segment && source.href.endsWith(segment) ? segment : source.label) : ''
---

<tr class="row">
  <th scope="row" class="row__cell row__cell--name" data-col="name">
    <a class="row__link" href={href}>
      <span class="visually-hidden">{project.data.title}, </span>{project.id}
    </a>
  </th>

  <td class="row__cell row__cell--status" data-col="status">
    <StatusTag status={status} />
  </td>

  <td class="row__cell row__cell--date" data-col="date">
    <time datetime={iso}>{iso}</time>
  </td>

  <td class="row__cell row__cell--stack" data-col="stack">
    {stack.join(' ')}
    {
      source && (
        <a class="row__repo" href={source.href} rel="noopener">
          <span class="visually-hidden">repository </span>{repoLabel}
        </a>
      )
    }
  </td>
</tr>

<style>
  .row__cell {
    vertical-align: baseline;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .row__cell--name {
    text-align: start;
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  /* Underlined, not only recoloured, so the row reads as a link on a monochrome
     display and under Windows high contrast. The underline is the affordance; the
     colour is the shortcut. */
  .row__link {
    color: var(--c-text);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }

  .row__link:hover {
    color: var(--c-link-hover);
  }

  /* The focus ring is the global `:focus-visible` two-tone outline, so it is a shape
     change and not a colour change, and it clears 5.15:1 even on the hover surface.
     `border-radius` only, so the ring is a crisp rectangle around the name rather than
     a pill around a whole row. */
  .row__link:focus-visible {
    border-radius: 1px;
  }

  /* A second link, a second treatment. It is not the row's subject, so it is not bold
     and it is not the content colour: it takes `--c-link`, which is a documented accent
     for exactly this and clears AA on the row's hover surface. It is underlined for the
     same reason the name is, because a link that is only a different hue is a link that
     disappears on a greyscale display. Two links in a row, two underlines, one of them
     bold — the difference is carried by weight and position, not by hue.

     The underline is also what stops this reading as a fifth technology. In a list of
     `rust gpui windows`, an undecorated `Sh_Images-Reborn` is just another word in a run of
     words, and the run is exactly the kind of thing a reader skims. Underlined, it is the
     only thing in the cell that goes somewhere, which is a shape difference rather than a
     colour one.

     The 1ch gap is a MARGIN on the anchor, not a character in the template, so a project
     with `links: []` cannot leave the separator behind without the link. One cell of
     whitespace is the whole cost, and it is the number in the frontmatter arithmetic. */
  .row__repo {
    margin-inline-start: 1ch;
    color: var(--c-link);
    font-weight: 400;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }

  .row__repo:hover {
    color: var(--c-link-hover);
  }

  .row__repo:focus-visible {
    border-radius: 1px;
  }

  .row__cell--date,
  .row__cell--status {
    color: var(--c-text-soft);
  }
</style>
